# Cloudflare Pages cache policy (ignored by GitHub Pages).
# Demo assets are content-stable; HTML revalidates via Pages defaults.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# The demo bundle is CONTENT-HASHED (/dist/demo.<hash>.min.css, generated by
# scripts/hash-asset.js on every `npm run build:demo-css`), so a byte change is a new
# URL and a cached copy is simply never requested again. That is what makes a one-year
# immutable TTL safe here — and it is required, not merely nice: at the old fixed
# filename this same TTL let a shipped CSS change stay invisible for up to 8 days, at
# the browser AND the Cloudflare edge, where neither a hard reload nor a private tab
# could shake it loose. Never point a page at an unhashed name in this directory.
# NB: matches the ROOT /dist/ only. The npm package bundles (/ui/base/dist/, /ui/card/dist/…)
# are deliberately unhashed — consumers link them by a stable name — and are not matched here.
# Referrer-Policy: carousel thumbnails are a CSS background — `background-image:
# var(--ui-carousel-thumb-url)`, where the url() is an absolute v4.browser.style/cdn-cgi
# transform set per slide in a style= attribute. A CSS-initiated fetch uses the
# STYLESHEET's referrer policy, not the document's, so the page's
# <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer"> never reaches it: Pages sets
# `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` on this response, the fetch sends the
# pages.dev origin, and the zone's Hotlink Protection 403s it. An <img srcset> on the same
# page is document-initiated, obeys the meta, and loads — which is why only the THUMBS went
# missing, and only on pages.dev (on the zone itself page and images are same-origin).
# A failed background-image is silent: nothing in the console says why.
/dist/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
# MCP discovery documents (see docs/mcp-poc.md). CORS `*` so clients can fetch
# them cross-origin; short TTL so manifest edits propagate within minutes.
# These rules cover the STATIC files only — the /mcp endpoint is a Pages
# Function and `_headers` never applies to Function responses; its CORS
# headers live in functions/mcp.js.
/.well-known/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
# The server card is extensionless (SEP-2127 path), so Pages cannot infer the
# required application/json content type from the filename.
/.well-known/mcp-server-card
  Content-Type: application/json
# HTTP-level MCP discovery hint on the root page; pairs with the <link> in index.html.
/
  Link: </.well-known/mcp-server-card>; rel="mcp-server-card"
